{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"expert/advanced-prompting","track":"engineers","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"Advanced Prompting","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":[]},"prerequisites":[],"previous":{"key":"expert/advanced-multiagent","number":24,"title":"Advanced Multi-Agent","drawn":false,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"expert/advanced-context-engineering","number":26,"title":"Advanced Context Engineering","drawn":false,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[],"body":null,"draft":{"summary":"Reasoning and prompting strategies that go well beyond the basics from Prompt Engineering.","parts":["Forcing structured output with constrained decoding","Reflexion","Tree of Thought","LLM Council","Caveman","Ponytail"],"written":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"forcing-structured-output","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Forcing Structured Output"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Asking for a format in the prompt works most of the time, but it is fragile. Constrained decoding guarantees the format instead. When the model picks its next token, the pool of possible tokens is filtered by a grammar, so only a token that keeps the output valid can be chosen. The one exception is an answer cut off at the length limit. Libraries such as Outlines and XGrammar do this, and most LLM APIs, local and cloud, support it out of the box."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/dottxt-ai/outlines"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Outlines"}]},{"type":"text","text":": structured outputs for LLMs"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/mlc-ai/xgrammar"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"XGrammar"}]},{"type":"text","text":": fast, flexible and portable structured generation"}]}]}]}]},"quiz":null}